[This is pretty much a derail from the original topic, since his post
doesn't answer any of the questions in the quoted text, but the advice may 
be helpful anyway.]

Hauling loot is the least important thing for a new character to worry
about.  Drop all that crap that looks like it's valuable but you can't
use yet; park it by the stairs on _each_ level if you're really low STR.
Focus on getting what you need to make it to the higher levels -- good
armor (AC0) and a decent weapon.

If you carry around scrolls and potions without a sack, you're simply inviting 
them to be smashed, boiled, burned, and frozen.  Dump those suckers.

> A suit of leather weighs in at 150, so the minimum wouldn't even allow 
> safely toting a single extra suit of armor for polypiling, cash, or just 
> getting out of the hands of monsters that can wear armor.

None of these are meaningful to an early-game character, though.

Carry extra suits of armor is pointless; all body armor is a temporary
holdover 'til you get mithril or DSM, neither of which rust and thus you
don't require a spare of them.  Carrying spare helmets and boots
is also pointless, since the Mines (and dungeon proper) are littered
with them; just pick another up off the floor if they get rusty.

Polypiling is absolutely not an early-game concern; each charge from a
wand is worth a huge amount, so you won't be doing that 'til the midgame
when you've accumulated a huge bunch of crap and can get the max return
for each wand zot.

Selling armor for cash is a lost cause when pet-thefting and
credit-cloning exist; do either of those, rather than trying to lug
heavy crud around for pennies.

Worrying about monsters wearing armor is pointless; just kill them.  Put
it this way: You have so many +hit bonuses as a player that the easiest 
way to find mithril in the Mines is to find the dwarf you actually miss
occasionally.  Monsters putting on scale mail isn't going to save them
one bit, or even allow you to notice.

> As an arbitrary comfort level, I want about 300gp minimum loot hauling 
> capacity, and I'll exercise if I don't have it. 

Just do Soko first.  All the boulder pushing will fix your STR fine, and
you won't have to worry about all the mid-game stuff you're trying to do
in the early-game.  If you are standing around exercising and not doing
anything else, this is a very bad scene.

> the amount of time spent.  I haven't done the math, but it might be 
> calculatable to figure out how much exercise time vs how much trip time 
> saved.

This is a classic case of missing the forest for the trees.

> Most of my games die before Mine Town, and out of the just over 500 games 
> I've played so far, I've seen only a handful of wishes, GoP, and BoH.

I'm going to suggest that most of your games die because you're focusing
on doing the wrong things in the early game.  Don't worry about
specifically exercising STR to carry junk -- you'll get enough exercise
just by trying to move forward through the game.

